{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"8766146d53644fc3","type":"group","x":-5000,"y":1520,"width":7240,"height":4587,"color":"4","label":"Robinson Crusoe"},
		{"id":"105e10ea2a65fc79","type":"group","x":-11280,"y":-1080,"width":3095,"height":6270,"color":"5","label":"The Stationeers and the story of copyright"},
		{"id":"cbcdc7650c867052","type":"group","x":-2400,"y":-4526,"width":3325,"height":5076,"color":"5","label":"Alexander Selkirk"},
		{"id":"711dc3bbb688c332","type":"group","x":-7600,"y":-8040,"width":3876,"height":3036,"label":"Defoe's youth and education"},
		{"id":"cec1d48054724399","type":"group","x":-7360,"y":-4838,"width":2271,"height":3156,"color":"4","label":"Defoe's political career"},
		{"id":"14a27043c474549c","type":"group","x":-7600,"y":-1280,"width":3098,"height":1496,"color":"4","label":"DEFOE IN 1709"},
		{"id":"ee65f44360a9cfb8","type":"group","x":-7360,"y":1360,"width":1620,"height":1820,"color":"4","label":"Defoe later life"},
		{"id":"41419b3bf4232893","type":"group","x":-4880,"y":-4600,"width":2030,"height":1400,"color":"5","label":"The original sea-faring writers"},
		{"id":"e63a3090b448a0c2","type":"group","x":-9935,"y":-5279,"width":1750,"height":1505,"color":"4","label":"Defoe's trades and writings"},
		{"id":"e0b47523c121acb6","type":"group","x":-7850,"y":4273,"width":1300,"height":1834,"label":"Story of buttons, fixed point"},
		{"id":"6d3549e4b28330a7","type":"group","x":-9645,"y":-3152,"width":1460,"height":1470,"color":"5","label":"True born englishman"},
		{"id":"a65a22b0f601a13e","type":"group","x":-4680,"y":-3040,"width":1412,"height":1272,"label":"the history of piracy"},
		{
			"id":"84b7dee32956b20b",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"They appear to have a youth program\n---\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NhE2D6j6sKA",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-9480,
			"y":3650,
			"width":343,
			"height":145,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"377ff2383c251a24",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"This is them now\n---\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=msvM68tx8To",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-9580,
			"y":3410,
			"width":260,
			"height":140,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"572fdca27aa9d3b5",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🚧 I'm sorry, the history of internet piracy is in another DLC, dedicated to the memory of Aaron Swartz.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-9260,
			"y":2900,
			"width":383,
			"height":140,
			"color":"3"
		},
		{
			"id":"ffa7e7874c15a21d",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Around the year 2000, with the rise of the internet and personal computers, the notion of copyright came into its biggest challenge since its infancy.\n---\nCorporate control was greatly threatened, but in a switch that echoes the one of 1709, they managed to develop a system based on subscriptions that would leave control firmly into corporate hands.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-9837,
			"y":2380,
			"width":398,
			"height":380,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"5001cc0d4b735921",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"In 2026, the Stationer's Company still exists.\n---\nThey are a networking hub for all things related to printing, journalism and media.\nThey are focused on education, training and event organizing.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-9960,
			"y":3040,
			"width":398,
			"height":220,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"d66f54211422ee81",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"2026 corporate members of the Stationeers include, among others...",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-10117,
			"y":4111,
			"width":743,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"a0bdf7fde38b0268",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Pearson\n---\nPearson is widely criticized for its financial and commercial ties to the infrastructure of illegal settlements in Palestine.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-10400,
			"y":4350,
			"width":380,
			"height":280
		},
		{
			"id":"9a6d72518f0572d0",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🏁 The August 1st 2024 report by Novara Media highlights The Independent as one of the worse offenders when it comes to bias in favor of war crimes and genocide.\n---\nTo this day, they continue to cover the middle east with a strong editorial position in favor of the ethnic cleansing colonial operations of the zionist army and settlers.\n",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-10640,
			"y":3723,
			"width":602,
			"height":250,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"0be41d82f0174394",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"👥 In 2026, the Master of the Worshipful Company of Stationers and Newspaper Makers is Doug Wills\n---\nHe held the role of group managing editor for the Evening Standard and The Independent.\nHe is now Editor Emeritus.\n",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-10117,
			"y":3390,
			"width":440,
			"height":260,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"fd869b587bb52b34",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Sun Chemical\n---\nSun Chemical is widely criticized for the ethical failures in its mineral supply chains, specifically regarding mica and cobalt.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-9500,
			"y":4331,
			"width":320,
			"height":199
		},
		{
			"id":"e2ae572bb5b1a69c",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Ricoh\n---\nRicoh is a key technology supplier to the Israeli Defense Forces (IDF). The company provides high-end digital hardware and services to military units operating in Gaza and the West Bank. ",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-9940,
			"y":4370,
			"width":360,
			"height":240
		},
		{
			"id":"58bb13966ecc434b",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Doug Wills is also the president of the Rotary club of London\n---\nThe Rotary Club is a volunteer network focused on promoting peace and fighting disease through an business-focused lens.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-10640,
			"y":3423,
			"width":362,
			"height":227
		},
		{
			"id":"cec087fa509286a3",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🚧 I'm sorry, the history of how the disney company and so many others evolved and abuse copyright law is in another DLC, which will be sure to shout out Nintendo.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-10380,
			"y":2430,
			"width":383,
			"height":140,
			"color":"3"
		},
		{
			"id":"cd0283e8195b2140",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"⏸️ This is how Press Your Buttons was born! \n---\nIt was trying to develop a new engine of non-linear storytelling.\nThis turned out to be way too ambitious for a single broken human, however.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-7830,
			"y":4333,
			"width":590,
			"height":160
		},
		{
			"id":"46d1d7d279695e90",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Heidelberg Materials\n---\nHeidelberg Materials is widely criticized for the \"pillage\" of natural resources in occupied Palestine, Africa and Indonesia.\n\nDespite recent claims of ceasing operations there, it remains on the UN Database of companies profiting from illegal settlements and the unlawful exploitation of Palestinian land.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-10880,
			"y":4350,
			"width":380,
			"height":320
		},
		{
			"id":"e7604054d900bb9a",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"For centuries since, the notion of copyright continued to prosper, with complex results.\n---\nIt is hard to measure whether the (deceptively) stated purpose of author protection did good commensurable to the bad done by corporate exploitation and gatekeeping of knowledge.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-9997,
			"y":1988,
			"width":398,
			"height":275,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"23279fa33d51e661",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"https://novaramedia.com/2024/08/01/we-ran-the-numbers-heres-how-britains-progressive-newspapers-have-covered-gaza/",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-10960,
			"y":3913,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"a248e24ff233c12a",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"https://www.whoprofits.org/companies/company/3840?heidelberg-materials-formerly-heidelbergcement-",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-11010,
			"y":4710,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"f9cff895b483e8c8",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cd702lxp838o",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-10260,
			"y":4730,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"1371beaea66b6240",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"https://youtu.be/-J_bVzF2OyE?t=16\nit helped me understand how truly and beautifully interconnected our world really is",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-11010,
			"y":3655,
			"width":360,
			"height":140
		},
		{
			"id":"710240b0715019c0",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"At first, I wrote two intertwined parabolas for the Fuck Capitalism Game Jam 2026.\n---\nI was really proud of them, but I got really worried about how they would be received.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-7460,
			"y":4573,
			"width":550,
			"height":160
		},
		{
			"id":"2b02d4f5b35e919c",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"So then I wrote two more.\n---\nBut it did not lessen the worry.\nThe world was quite clear where it stood.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-7457,
			"y":4773,
			"width":550,
			"height":120
		},
		{
			"id":"9edb75a34c884676",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Tech companies like Microsoft are at the center of this system.\n---\nFor instance, Microsoft took ownership of Minecraft from Notch, or Fable from Peter Molyneux.\n\nWhy am I talking about this? \nWell, may I interest you in...",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-9220,
			"y":4863,
			"width":440,
			"height":267
		},
		{
			"id":"901196f46ed7d163",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Did someone say mining?\n---\nIf you want a hopeful epilogue, how about...",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-9900,
			"y":5010,
			"width":592,
			"height":120,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"b1f06efe9345b693",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"👥 Craven Peyton\n---\nA staunch Whig MP. His presence on the committee signaled that this was a \"Revolutionary\" piece of law—meant to break the old-school censorship models associated with the Stuart kings and replace them with Enlightenment property rights.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-10580,
			"y":1950,
			"width":400,
			"height":260,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"c1b7e3aa388bec2c",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"By 2026, copyright was so well established that even anticapitalist game jams did not dare to challenge it.\n---\nA reminder of its arbitrariness and its benefactor was sorely needed.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-8720,
			"y":4900,
			"width":460,
			"height":220
		},
		{
			"id":"f2d12a1a13bd95c8",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"👥 Edward Wortley Montagu\n---\nHe was the \"front man.\" He officially introduced the bill. He was a wealthy Whig, highly educated, and a close friend of the great writers of the era (Addison and Steele). His goal was to move literature away from \"guild secrets\" and into a transparent, market-oriented system.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-10990,
			"y":1890,
			"width":400,
			"height":280,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"c297971b0aa5ac0f",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Memoirs of a Cavalier (1720) mixes the life of Andrew Newport to various war memoirs.\n---\nIt was published anonymously under the pretense of being a forgotten manuscript found in a closet.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-7285,
			"y":1940,
			"width":345,
			"height":210
		},
		{
			"id":"0f93c2c1e39524ab",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Moll Flanders (1722) is the story of Elizabeth Adkins, a notorious London thief.\n---\nDefoe got a lot of details from meeting the actual criminal in prison.\nHe added a lot of scandalous fictionalizations (bigamy, prostitution) that would warrant a separate feminist analysis.\nIt sounds like the kind of defamation that could really impact the person's life, you know.\nAt least Elizabeth survived him.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-7240,
			"y":2210,
			"width":380,
			"height":370
		},
		{
			"id":"a8f5434158d26eeb",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"What is a parabola?\n---\nA parabola is a simple, realistic story used to illustrate a deeper moral, spiritual, or philosophical lesson.\nThis genre is closely tied to religion.\nI never realized it before now, but I also write parabolas.\nIn a sense, this is a sort of parabola. Though it's not exactly simple...",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-7150,
			"y":4299,
			"width":580,
			"height":194
		},
		{
			"id":"d44490ea1420a52c",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"So I dug and I dug deep down in the Content Mines, looking for a way to make things work.\n---\nBut I live in Capitalism, and every rock I looked under had more exploitation, plagiarism and oppression.\n\nYet, amidst so much man-made cruelty and suffering, so far down the mines, i saw traces of the truth, of reality, of the genuine people burrowed beneath centuries of silent oppression.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-7457,
			"y":4933,
			"width":550,
			"height":280
		},
		{
			"id":"e56458bd0df6f92f",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"After the success of Robinson Crusoe, Daniel Defoe was really determined to milk the \"fake autobiography\" cash cow.\n---\nHe went on to write 7 major books and a couple of famous non-fiction.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-7260,
			"y":1400,
			"width":400,
			"height":261,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"b2fdcf2cf43e6f3e",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Did Defoe ever discuss identity fraud?\n---\nHe wrote a lot about the importance of honest business, but he is also known to have used at least 200 pen names.\nIt appears that his conception of ethics was grounded in financial gain and property.\nThanks to him, books had become legitimate property, but it seems that people's lives had not.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6430,
			"y":1693,
			"width":440,
			"height":300
		},
		{
			"id":"537ebcc545e8624c",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"The King of Pirates (1720) and Captain Singleton (1720) ripped off the story of Henry Avery.\n--- \nAnd various minor Caribbean pirates.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-7340,
			"y":1730,
			"width":480,
			"height":180
		},
		{
			"id":"e857447d4711f6a4",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"He left behind an unfinished manuscript of moral guidance titled The Compleat English Gentleman.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6835,
			"y":2800,
			"width":380,
			"height":100
		},
		{
			"id":"4cf1af5a464528e7",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"https://archive.org/details/compleatenglishg00defouoft",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6775,
			"y":2960,
			"width":260,
			"height":80
		},
		{
			"id":"9cccf036f79f5693",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"A Journal of the Plague Year (1722) is a retelling of his uncle Henry's journals.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6915,
			"y":1940,
			"width":400,
			"height":105
		},
		{
			"id":"4e6d0241b01d0f5e",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Roxana (1724) was modeled on scandalous mistresses of king Charles II.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6860,
			"y":2090,
			"width":400,
			"height":90
		},
		{
			"id":"2e1ab5221c315e9d",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🏁 Daniel Defoe died on April 24, 1731\n---\nThe recorded cause of death, \"lethargy\", hints at a stroke.\nIn financial trouble, he was hiding from creditors in a rented room not far from where he was born.\nHe left behind him a wife and 6 surviving children.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6690,
			"y":2395,
			"width":480,
			"height":200,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"5aefe148e3597224",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"I studied the history of copyright and the life of Daniel Defoe.\n---\nI genuinely thought that at least I'd read about travels, go all over the world in a time I didn't know.\nI had no idea what kind of man he was.\n\nA man like all the others.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-7455,
			"y":5253,
			"width":545,
			"height":240
		},
		{
			"id":"2dbf95ffd5c351e3",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Somehow, everything fit into place. \n---\nThings paralleled and mirrored each other in a story too perfect to stay untold.\n\nIt was theft all the way down.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-7455,
			"y":5533,
			"width":550,
			"height":160
		},
		{
			"id":"1e277176503464f4",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Wait, how was he debt-ridden if his books were so successful? \n---\nHe was spectacularly bad at managing his money and frequently invested in \"get rich quick\" schemes that did not pan out.\nIn 1729, an old debt resurfaced with decades of interest and legal fees.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6425,
			"y":2880,
			"width":480,
			"height":240
		},
		{
			"id":"f645ae2d53659672",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"The godfather of copyright turned out to be the perfect allegory of capitalist hypocrisy.\n---\nIn a way, his life is now one of these parables he loved so much.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6280,
			"y":2080,
			"width":420,
			"height":200,
			"color":"2"
		},
		{
			"id":"316561ba57437d46",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"So I took my parabolas and hid them deep, deep down in the content mines.\n---\nAnd maybe some day someone would find them, like I found the story of Will and Robin.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-7455,
			"y":5733,
			"width":550,
			"height":180
		},
		{
			"id":"c8166b12f5fd3a3b",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"So how about you?\n---\nAre you up for digging?",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-7330,
			"y":5973,
			"width":300,
			"height":120,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{"id":"d674c3e21ca88716","type":"text","text":"⭐ The Life of Daniel Defoe\n---\nOne of those names you probably know so well from having heard it a thousand times, so you don't even notice or pause to wonder anymore.","x":-3760,"y":2210,"width":420,"height":220,"color":"4"},
		{
			"id":"fec183939ec99fce",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"ok, but remind me what goes on in the book please\n---\nWell its the story of a middle class English man who runs away from home and gets enslaved and works in plantations.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4230,
			"y":2608,
			"width":370,
			"height":192,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"c6af20895de1c73a",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Wait what? How did publishing work before 1709?\n---\nBooks were simply manufactured objects sold by printing companies.\n\nAuthors sold them their manuscript once and for all.\n\nMost authors, and more largely artists, either had patrons or were self-funded.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-7320,
			"y":-220,
			"width":580,
			"height":220,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"3ec1da4e2885b806",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"However, other cartels like the Stationeers had much better luck protecting their monopoly.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-7380,
			"y":-580,
			"width":260,
			"height":140,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"deaf19e215f1f4a6",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Around 1709, Daniel Defoe began working for the Royal African Company.\n---\nHe received financial stake in the company in exchange for works in public relations and lobbying.\nHe wrote many pamphlets and extensive coverage in his personal newspaper.\nHe was what we would nowadays call a dedicated spin doctor.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6260,
			"y":-1080,
			"width":390,
			"height":360,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"0e9ad9634dcf6048",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🧱 Although the text is now public domain, all the versions I've been able to find are behind publisher paywall\n\nhttps://www.cambridge.org/core/books/abs/daniel-defoe-in-context/defoe-and-slavery/66642B11A08FCC27C25E0E5DC506A5DE",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6480,
			"y":-640,
			"width":345,
			"height":240,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"6bf4d15935fded99",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🏴‍☠️ Around 1709, Daniel Defoe's \"Review\" turned its attention to the question of piracy\n---\nNo, not the one in the sea.\nUpset about his financial loss of opportunity cost in his previous experiences, he started claiming a bold new idea: a BOOK should be the AUTHOR’s Property.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5705,
			"y":-1020,
			"width":390,
			"height":300,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"509848ca1071f626",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🚧 I'm sorry, The Growing Inaccessibility Of Science is in another DLC",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4913,
			"y":-132,
			"width":260,
			"height":115,
			"color":"3"
		},
		{
			"id":"92aa118063569517",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🗝️ Any source in particular?\n---\nHis writing at the time, but if you want something precise, check out Daniel Defoe's The Review, Vol. VI, No. 101 on November 10, 1709.\nYou can find a few extracts around that time from the man himself by searching online.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5133,
			"y":-675,
			"width":480,
			"height":200
		},
		{
			"id":"8f19d9b72a1aaf6e",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Daniel Defoe's \"Review\" had moderate popularity, but it would go on to have tremendous political impact.\n---\nIt was a controversial and scandalous publication, often a \"hate-read\".\nBut his \"shitposting\" was in the right place in the right time.\nThe Stationeers thought they had found here a useful idiot, but their plans would backfire...",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6340,
			"y":-320,
			"width":460,
			"height":320
		},
		{
			"id":"e42d263507160c9c",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"The 1710s were a tumultuous period for Daniel Defoe.\n---\nHe continued his ambiguous position between the two rival political parties, but playing both sides was a risky game.\nHe would be forced to shut down his newspaper, and his stressful life culminated in a stroke in 1715.\nNearing 60, he retired to focus on fiction.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5705,
			"y":-132,
			"width":390,
			"height":300,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"546364afd60c631e",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"It eventually turned into the Statute of Anne, which went into application on April 10th, 1710.\n---\nIt is widely regarded as the birth of copyright.\nIt did grant the Stationeers all the monopolistic control over enforcement they were hoping for.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-10000,
			"y":850,
			"width":400,
			"height":320,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"66de9503354cb60a",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"This was the birth of what we now know as public domain.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-10980,
			"y":1650,
			"width":260,
			"height":140,
			"color":"2"
		},
		{
			"id":"6ca88579b6cc0c82",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Not to mention the realities of the means of production.\n---\nThe Stationeers owned the presses and the publishing companies.\n\nThey argued in favor of a right of ownership for the author of texts, mostly so that authors could sell it back to them.\n\nThis is what happened in practice, as authors did not have any alternative to turn to.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-10525,
			"y":850,
			"width":450,
			"height":320,
			"color":"6"
		},
		{
			"id":"8239acc3dc32054e",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"In 1734, Hogarth's Act extended the Statute of Anne to images.\n---\nIt was focused on engraving.\n",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-10180,
			"y":1390,
			"width":365,
			"height":200
		},
		{
			"id":"fa54a1018421f607",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Who were the people behind this change?\n---\nWho made the Stationeers' plans backfire?",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-10670,
			"y":1650,
			"width":300,
			"height":180
		},
		{
			"id":"b71b1b9d35701f92",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"The Royal African Company was an English joint-stock company that held a monopoly on the transatlantic slave trade.\n---\nIn 1709, its royal monopoly was under attack by independent merchants.\nDespite Defoe's support, the monopolists would lose this battle and slave trade would be open to competition.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-7080,
			"y":-1020,
			"width":340,
			"height":340
		},
		{
			"id":"e302cb83277536b7",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🧱 Where can I read the primary source?\n---\nAlthough the content of old periodicals are in the public domain by now, only rare digitized extracts are available to the public.\nMost of them are behind the paywall of private publishers like <a href=\"https://www.jstor.org/stable/20779451\" target=\"_blank\">JSTOR</a> or <a href=\"https://www.taylorfrancis.com/books/mono/10.4324/9781003576228/defoe-review-1704-13-volume-6-1709-10-part-john-mcveagh\" target=\"_blank\">Taylor & Francis</a>.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5008,
			"y":-425,
			"width":340,
			"height":226
		},
		{
			"id":"c80512e49161e014",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🚧 I'm sorry, the history of slavery and human ownership is in another DLC.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-7000,
			"y":-570,
			"width":241,
			"height":130,
			"color":"3"
		},
		{
			"id":"c026b7ef76f5979a",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"In 1719, Daniel Defoe published Robinson Crusoe.\n---\nIt was a scandalous controversy, a phenomenal commercial success, and a major step in the history of literature.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6485,
			"y":600,
			"width":650,
			"height":140,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"b0fbffef408d52b4",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"This arrangement of monopoly and censorship was set to expire after 150 years.\n---\nWhen it did, the world had changed and the parliament was not eager to continue an explicit monopolistic control of the presses.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-10500,
			"y":-190,
			"width":400,
			"height":240,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"42f5b7c23e266577",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"https://www.stationers.org/news/features/the-stationers-company-charter\n\nhttps://www.copyrighthistory.org/cam/commentary/uk_1557/uk_1557_com_972007121517.html\n\nhttps://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Royal_Charter_of_the_Company_of_Stationers",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-10020,
			"y":-210,
			"width":380,
			"height":260,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"34a94a9248353b73",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"For years, the Stationeers desperately tried to renew their privileges in vain.\n---\nIn 1709, they found in Daniel Defoe's arguments the key to their success.\nThey decided to change their approach.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-10500,
			"y":110,
			"width":400,
			"height":224,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"80f55566a9214428",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"On December 12, 1709, a consortium of influential Stationeers addressed a petition to the House of Commons.\n---\nIt was a vibrant plea for authors to be the owner of their text.\nPeople were puzzled by the change of heart, but nobody suspected their true intentions.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-10000,
			"y":490,
			"width":400,
			"height":320,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"0887cfb787808796",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"What was their new strategy?\n---\nThey would plead in favor of the authors who needed protection from illicit copies.\n\nBut to apply this kind of protection, a registry would need to be kept of who the true author of each work was.\n\nAnd of course, they would be the ones in charge of the registry, in fact in their long expertise of royal censorship they already had one.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-10525,
			"y":490,
			"width":450,
			"height":320,
			"color":"6"
		},
		{
			"id":"bfb575b0e0e43a90",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"👥 The Political Lobbyist: Abel Roper.\n---\nRoper was a Tory bookseller with deep ties to the political class. He knew how to frame the argument so it sounded like \"public interest\" rather than \"corporate greed.\"",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-10900,
			"y":630,
			"width":320,
			"height":220,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"ea7e026faac10d96",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"But there's one thing that the Stationeers had not counted on.\n---\nThe progressive politicians who drafted the bills introduced an expiry date for the ownership of text by their authors. \nThey chose 14 years, based on the patent law of the time.\nAfter this duration, the copyright expired.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-10740,
			"y":1290,
			"width":440,
			"height":260,
			"color":"6"
		},
		{
			"id":"972f886298c5ae95",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Statute_of_Anne",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-9540,
			"y":1290,
			"width":260,
			"height":60,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"ef2813dd2fc1deef",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"The Minecraft DLC",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-8213,
			"y":6607,
			"width":415,
			"height":160,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{"id":"73905d275c7784ba","type":"file","file":"web_servers/buttons/assets/minecraft_dlc.canvas","x":-8205,"y":6887,"width":400,"height":400,"color":"1"},
		{
			"id":"acd2497ed65d7e7d",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"But at the turn of the century, the old system was in trouble.\n---\nThe old royal monopoly on printing had expired, and the parliament argued for years over a more modern solution.\n\nOf course, the Stationeers did not want to let go of their power.\n\nThe concepts floated by Daniel Defoe would give them the idea for an ingenious new system. \n\nBut that's a whole other story...",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-8785,
			"y":366,
			"width":580,
			"height":387,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"21ae235f7ba5636b",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"The Worshipful Company of Stationers and Newspaper Makers was formed in 1403.\n---\nIt was the typical case of a corporate guild of bookmakers and booksellers.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-10500,
			"y":-850,
			"width":400,
			"height":200,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"fc3720924a3fe3d2",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"On the May 4, 1557, the company received a royal charter granting them a monopoly on book production.\n---\nIt gave the company complete control over what could be printed or confiscated.\n\nThis was a mutually beneficial agreement for the crown, as it allowed complete control over publications.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-10500,
			"y":-590,
			"width":400,
			"height":340,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"25622f4a8cef59e9",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"👥 The Mastermind: John Baskett.\n---\nAs the \"King’s Printer\" and a legendary monopolist, he had the most to lose. He was the political heavyweight who understood that they needed a new legal \"hook\" to stop the pirate printers in the north and in Scotland.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-11260,
			"y":630,
			"width":320,
			"height":220,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"820a7941a48605ab",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Decrees in 1586 and 1637 make their role of censors on behalf or the crown quite explicit.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-10882,
			"y":-190,
			"width":260,
			"height":140
		},
		{
			"id":"3a50e1168a323b41",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Who designed this new approach?",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-11080,
			"y":430,
			"width":420,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"8fd6b26d7a09eb3e",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"⭐ The joint stories of the Stationeers and copyright",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-10500,
			"y":-1010,
			"width":400,
			"height":100,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"6d889bbbf0bab92e",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"⏰ 1709 you say?",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4898,
			"y":-1080,
			"width":245,
			"height":75,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{"id":"d0c1f86e10f949da","type":"text","text":"Robinson Crusoe?\n---\nRobinson Crusoe is an English adventure novel by Daniel Defoe, first published on 25 April 1719.\nMost people are familiar with its narrative of a sailor shipwrecked on a wild island.\nBut few people even know the story of the story.","x":-3000,"y":2120,"width":750,"height":200,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"d59c4d2f4f4674df","type":"text","text":"Strange surprizing adventures...\n---\nLike the one you're living right now!","x":-3130,"y":3220,"width":260,"height":140,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"e8a4775851bc5327","type":"text","text":"📷 That's not the title of the book, though...\n---\nIts original title was:\n\"The\nLIFE\nand\nStrange Surprizing \nADVENTURES\nof \nRobinson Crusoe\nof York, Mariner:\n\nWho lived Eight and Twenty Years, all alone in an un-inhabited Island on the Coast of America, near the Mouth of the Great River of Oroonoque;\nHaving been cast on Shore by Shipwreck, wherein all the Men perished but himself. \n\nWITH\nAn Account how he was at last as strangely deliver'd by Pyrates. \nWritten by Himself.\"\n\nYou can see it for yourself on <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Robinson_Crusoe_1719_1st_edition.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Wikimedia.\n<img src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1e/Robinson_Crusoe_1719_1st_edition.jpg\" /></a>","x":-2920,"y":2620,"width":440,"height":460,"color":"6"},
		{
			"id":"178ee6244499988f",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"He hid from Spanish sailors who stopped to pee under the very tree he was hiding in.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1880,
			"y":-1946,
			"width":260,
			"height":135
		},
		{"id":"title","type":"text","text":"⭐ The true story of the story of Robinson Crusoe\n---","x":-2000,"y":1806,"width":300,"height":200,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"6a342d3d556dcaa3","type":"text","text":"What do you mean, the story of the story?\n---\nI mean the story of how the book came to be, and the story of its author.","x":-2000,"y":2210,"width":300,"height":200,"color":"0"},
		{"id":"ed26200129d229b8","type":"text","text":"👥 Printed for W. Taylor","x":-2327,"y":3250,"width":230,"height":100,"color":"0"},
		{"id":"99677d0088771e0d","type":"text","text":"📍 at the Shop in Pater-Noster-Row","x":-2057,"y":3250,"width":200,"height":120,"color":"0"},
		{"id":"72d5074d19b63713","type":"text","text":"You forgot the part at the bottom...","x":-2277,"y":3110,"width":300,"height":80,"color":"0"},
		{
			"id":"a8d38e0524f7896d",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"What kind of government?\n---\nThe politics at the time were an intense war between the \"progressive\" Whigs and the \"traditionalist\" Tories.\nThe balance of power was a constant tug-of-war.\nDaniel Defoe's positions were closer to the Whigs, but he was spying for the Tories.\nAs a result, he was widely distrusted and seen as a double agent.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6950,
			"y":-1963,
			"width":470,
			"height":252,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"c78c58970d985108",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🚧 I'm sorry, the Act of Union is in another DLC",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5737,
			"y":-2189,
			"width":200,
			"height":133,
			"color":"3"
		},
		{
			"id":"4ee260ef31d9900b",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"\"France?\" What does France have to do with it?\n---\nDefoe was a fervent admirer of Louis XIV's militaristic centralism.\nBy talking about France, he could criticize the English government with a veneer of deniability.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5570,
			"y":-2484,
			"width":464,
			"height":220
		},
		{
			"id":"8725a0235c1a2659",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Daniel Defoe was quite upset that his poem had been widely copied.\n---\nIt appears that the majority of copies in circulation were cheap unauthorized versions on which he did not make any profit.\nHe claimed that up to 80000 copies were circulated, a staggering amount for the time.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-9557,
			"y":-2183,
			"width":460,
			"height":232,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"5b9234d08f40366a",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🗝️ In 1701, Daniel Defoe published The True-Born Englishman.\n---\nAt 41, he published this satirical poem against the xenophobic culture of England of the time.\nIt was aimed at defending the dutch born king, William III.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-9450,
			"y":-3070,
			"width":400,
			"height":280,
			"color":"2"
		},
		{
			"id":"8e055b600367b97e",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"In essence, a pro-immigration plea?\n---\nYes,  mostly focused on calling racial purity a myth, and pointing out shameful origins of established aristocrats.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-9050,
			"y":-2762,
			"width":350,
			"height":213
		},
		{
			"id":"05a22844cd0ac569",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Daniel Defoe's True-Born Englishman was a massive hit.\n---\nFirst published anonymously, the demand for it grew quickly, and many unauthorized copies were produced.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-9624,
			"y":-2606,
			"width":480,
			"height":187,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"ebf764b1093a8127",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"I do like this quote:\n---\nA true-born Englishman's a contradiction,  \nIn speech an irony, in fact a fiction;",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-9005,
			"y":-2514,
			"width":260,
			"height":230,
			"color":"6"
		},
		{
			"id":"d558c210ac6f1acc",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🚰 Daniel Defoe's \"The Shortest-Way with the Dissenter\"\n---\nOn the <a href=\"https://archive.org/details/shortest-way-dissenters\">Internet Archive</a>\n<iframe src=\"https://archive.org/details/shortest-way-dissenters\"></iframe>",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6870,
			"y":-2585,
			"width":490,
			"height":271
		},
		{
			"id":"833cbd5359bf31db",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"The king was quite pleased with the way Daniel Defoe defended him\n---\nDaniel Defoe became somewhat of an advisor to him in the last years of his life.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6567,
			"y":-3560,
			"width":375,
			"height":224,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"be08d3a2e0fb21b3",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"King William III died in 1702, his wife, Queen Anne, succeeded him to the throne.\n---\nShe was not close with Daniel Defoe. In fact she had strong ties to the Church of England.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6445,
			"y":-3305,
			"width":340,
			"height":268,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"f2c4a78c2f9975be",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Until he became a spy for the government!\n---\nThe Tory minister Robert Harley bailed him out of prison on condition that Daniel Defoe work for him.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6670,
			"y":-2237,
			"width":380,
			"height":230,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"9f04098fd76affe4",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"👥 Robert Harley",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6175,
			"y":-1963,
			"width":180,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"1eee3e89560bf812",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"👥 John Tutchin\n\nA radical political journalist. \nhttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Tutchin",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-8830,
			"y":-1957,
			"width":260,
			"height":240
		},
		{
			"id":"15ffce56be6fb0f6",
			"type":"text",
			"text":" 🚰 The True-Born Englishman?\n ---\n You can read the full text on <a href=\"https://gutenberg.org/ebooks/30159\">Project Gutenberg</a>.\n \n <iframe src=\"https://gutenberg.org/ebooks/30159\"></iframe>",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-8690,
			"y":-2618,
			"width":340,
			"height":283
		},
		{
			"id":"c0925e9d6778c0a2",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🚰 John Tutchin's \"The Foreigners\"\n---\nOn the <a href=\"https://archive.org/details/bim_early-english-books-1641-1700_the-foreigners-a-poem-_tutchin-john_1700/page/4/mode/2up\">Internet Archive</a>\n<iframe src=\"https://archive.org/details/bim_early-english-books-1641-1700_the-foreigners-a-poem-_tutchin-john_1700/page/4/mode/2up\"></iframe>",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-8530,
			"y":-1957,
			"width":265,
			"height":240
		},
		{
			"id":"4aa765f37fcebc89",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🚧 I'm sorry, Louis XIV is in another DLC",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5510,
			"y":-2159,
			"width":200,
			"height":133,
			"color":"3"
		},
		{
			"id":"20daebf4571d973f",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"In Prison, he started his own private publication.\n---\nA newsletter if you will.\n<a href=\"https://archive.org/details/bub_gb_El8TAAAAQAAJ\" target=\"_blank\">\"A Review of the Affairs of France\"</a> started as a weekly chronicle that shifted according to Daniel Defoe's interests.\n<iframe src=\"https://archive.org/details/bub_gb_El8TAAAAQAAJ\"></iframe>",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5967,
			"y":-2852,
			"width":460,
			"height":267,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"f033b3b0f313a483",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"After the Union Act, Daniel Defoe spent a lot of time in Edinburgh spying for the government.\n---\nHe monitored political dissent on the ground.\nAs the Union settled, he turned his attention to domestic affairs.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5970,
			"y":-2026,
			"width":390,
			"height":297,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"5929c71a78c15ca1",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Daniel Defoe and his \"review\" were instrumental in the redacting of the Act of Union in 1707\n---\nYou know, the creation of a \"United Kingdom\".\nBut this is a story for another DLC.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6218,
			"y":-2550,
			"width":398,
			"height":180,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"10818a63bbc83eb9",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Prison?",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6265,
			"y":-2324,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"53e72cec6b7fa897",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Spying?",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6195,
			"y":-2182,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"f2bca80414c0463c",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"When Dampier's ship approached Will, a Miskito crew member named Robin was the first to greet him, in a ritualistic embrace.\n---\nIt is suspected to be the origin for the name Robinson Crusoe.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1320,
			"y":-1998,
			"width":420,
			"height":312,
			"color":"2"
		},
		{
			"id":"8503bbea5714258d",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🏁 Alexander Selkirk did not survive the outbreak of yellow fever that broke through the ship.\n---\nHe did not have children. \nHe was buried at sea at 45.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":80,
			"y":-786,
			"width":490,
			"height":220,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"10c2d3659f2925de",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"The Miskito population is now split between Nicaragua and Honduras, mostly in North Caribbean Coast Autonomous Region.\n\nhttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North_Caribbean_Coast_Autonomous_Region",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-320,
			"y":-1806,
			"width":500,
			"height":240
		},
		{
			"id":"e693ab12b82102e0",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🚧 I'm sorry, the Miskito history is in another DLC",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-140,
			"y":-1486,
			"width":200,
			"height":133,
			"color":"3"
		},
		{
			"id":"c8b6312e204e71b1",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"\nIn 1885, a self-made net manufacturer, descendant of his brother erected a bronze statue to his memory in his hometown.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":105,
			"y":-406,
			"width":440,
			"height":140,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"7c503ef2307d7244",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"In 2023, a google map review shares how much of a comfort this statue has been through 55 years of life...\nhttps://maps.app.goo.gl/VmUF2Xb33mxgRy6H7",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-35,
			"y":-126,
			"width":360,
			"height":160,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"d0280adeca4940b9",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Will was Miskito.\n---\nThe Miskito were an indigenous people from Central America who allied with the British against the Spanish colonial settlers.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-790,
			"y":-2002,
			"width":450,
			"height":173
		},
		{
			"id":"bd486b17b7b63c96",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"He worried that he would die like this.\n---\nAnd that his feline companions would end up eating him.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1600,
			"y":-1846,
			"width":260,
			"height":160
		},
		{
			"id":"33ce6262cbd1326f",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"As they stopped to refill on a small deserted island, Alexander Selkirk had a fight with Captain Thomas Stradling.\n---\nAlexander wanted to do maintenance on the ship. \nThomas said it wasn't necessary.\nAlexander said he'd rather stay alone on the island than risking sailing off in this state.\nThomas took him at his word.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1979,
			"y":-3125,
			"width":400,
			"height":359,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"8d6b75f1d725745d",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"In 1831, Thomas Roscoe was one of the first person to connect the story of this Will from Dampier's journals to the character of Friday in Robinson Crusoe.\n---\nIt is a common understanding that Will is the inspiration from the character of Friday, despite one being shipwrecked and the other a native.\n\nIt should be stressed that the Miskito have never had anything to do with cannibalism, and a great deal of racist conflations were done by everyone involved.\n\nIt is not known to what measure Daniel Defoe was writing out of racist ignorance, out of deliberate sensationalist marketing, or out of propagandist political agenda.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-860,
			"y":-1766,
			"width":470,
			"height":480,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"7861245cc5620df9",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"👥 Thomas Roscoe",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-600,
			"y":-1206,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"38ce23dbc4abf051",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"👥 Robin",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1570,
			"y":-1419,
			"width":260,
			"height":60,
			"color":"2"
		},
		{
			"id":"26d00467d9154085",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🏁 Fictional happy ending\n---\nSince there are no historical accounts of their lives, at least we can imagine Will and Robin happy together at sea, dying of scurvy while trying to push colonial settlers away from their homeland.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1300,
			"y":-1592,
			"width":380,
			"height":213,
			"color":"2"
		},
		{
			"id":"62f48efa23a1d79f",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pjfYGsKJ-Qg",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1700,
			"y":-1606,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{"id":"a368a7df76d99d0d","type":"file","file":"web_servers/buttons/assets/AI model bias comparison.md","x":-1080,"y":-1146,"width":400,"height":400,"color":"1"},
		{
			"id":"4985caa70efc35f7",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Alexander Selkirk would have to survive all alone for 4 years.\n---\nHe befriended a population of feral cats that would keep him company and keep rodents away from his supplies.\nHe had to hide from passing enemy vessels while hoping for rescue.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1970,
			"y":-2581,
			"width":420,
			"height":270,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"f1f51c829d144f82",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/55538",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1980,
			"y":-602,
			"width":330,
			"height":120
		},
		{
			"id":"4c90dc1074065680",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Alexander Selkirk did not enjoy the fame.\n---\nHe did not like being a curiosity.\n\nHe would reportedly remark \"I am now worth eight hundred pounds, but shall never be as happy as when I was not worth a farthing\".",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1900,
			"y":-406,
			"width":500,
			"height":240,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"52d13464d929336f",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Within a year, Edward Cooke and Woodes Rogers had both published their account of the story.\n---",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1800,
			"y":-906,
			"width":460,
			"height":240,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"180be824b8cb2ff1",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"⏰ In 1720, as Robinson Crusoe was becoming a bestseller, Alexander Selkirk quietly shipped out on the HMS Weymouth off the coast of Africa.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1895,
			"y":214,
			"width":485,
			"height":260,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"f669887e913cc68b",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"👥 20 years earlier, a pirate named Will was abandoned on this island.\n---\nHe was too slow when his crew ran away from Spanish soldiers.\n\nHe would survive a few years on his own, before being rescued by William Dampier in 1684.\n\nNot much is known about him, apart from a brief mention in Dampier's journals.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1340,
			"y":-2609,
			"width":440,
			"height":298,
			"color":"2"
		},
		{
			"id":"1ae63739b63c26cf",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"https://www.google.com/maps/place/Isla+Robinson+Crusoe/@-33.6397812,-78.8460569,3a,15y,318.42h,77.29t/data=!3m8!1e1!3m6!1sCIHM0ogKEICAgIDT-euCKg!2e10!3e11!6shttps:%2F%2Flh3.googleusercontent.com%2Fgpms-cs-s%2FABJJf51T0ZaeyCLM1mCdbw4Tca4f3K097Jn4nuPHQRGn60Wk89Y6CDWsrCXdPO0_NHN8Tg_FcykUqC_lZGGWPge_V1WWX7i-0WSCr_hK9n-Cqmi78S64eLk2dWsvvFQBQKPIRZddq681%3Dw900-h600-k-no-pi12.71353919612109-ya318.42379835851926-ro0-fo100!7i8192!8i4096!4m12!1m5!3m4!2zMzPCsDM4JzI5LjAiUyA3OMKwNTAnMjguMCJX!8m2!3d-33.6413889!4d-78.8411111!3m5!1s0x96ff956094057d0d:0x7fd95ad2e2c9752!8m2!3d-33.6738424!4d-78.9363924!16s%2Fg%2F11j0h_t07r?hl=en&entry=ttu&g_ep=EgoyMDI2MDUwNi4wIKXMDSoASAFQAw%3D%3D",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-760,
			"y":-2780,
			"width":380,
			"height":240
		},
		{
			"id":"b08f9a775e13eb15",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"The Juan Fernandez island, also known as Más a Tierra, would be officially renamed Robinson Crusoe Island in 1966\n---\nhttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robinson_Crusoe_Island",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-460,
			"y":-2423,
			"width":360,
			"height":255
		},
		{
			"id":"d735c08134aa72b0",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Will_(Indian)",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1470,
			"y":-2106,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"dfad503cfd74c9d8",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"He went on to have a complicated love life and ended up joining the navy.\n---\nWhat little was left of his wealth got caught in legal domestic battles.\n\nFor the second time in his life, three decades and a whole story older, he took to the sea to run away from his troubles.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1895,
			"y":-106,
			"width":490,
			"height":280,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"4002dae8248d4756",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"👥 Edward Cooke",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1370,
			"y":-326,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"26cf16be5a4e1cdc",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/A_Voyage_to_the_South_Sea,_and_Round_the_World",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1340,
			"y":-596,
			"width":420,
			"height":190
		},
		{"id":"title_aside","type":"text","text":"The story of a white man?\n---\nAlas, that is where we must start.\n  Humans do love their stories to be character-centered.\n  But fear not, you will see many characters if you press the right buttons.","x":-1540,"y":2210,"width":300,"height":200,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"49209c0bf5486f14","type":"text","text":"I can't help but wonder if this psychological bias in stories reinforces a hyper-individualistic society.\n---","x":-1390,"y":2520,"width":300,"height":200,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"title_aside2","type":"text","text":"Though I guess a lot of them were white men.\n---\nSorry, its mostly their story.\n  But it doesn't have to be about them.","x":-1110,"y":2310,"width":300,"height":200,"color":"0"},
		{
			"id":"129c1ea38464bd60",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"I know...\n---\nIt's probably more than they deserve.\nIn fact this story is precisely about them getting more than one deserves.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-930,
			"y":2610,
			"width":260,
			"height":220,
			"color":"2"
		},
		{"id":"f919133328a5e0d3","type":"text","text":"Robinson Crusoe was a groundbreaking book...\n---\nFor quite a lot of reasons. \n\nEven without getting into the story of the story.","x":-50,"y":3328,"width":550,"height":152,"color":"5"},
		{
			"id":"ecbf5cec60c58bf2",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"The True-Born Englishman was way more successful than the original poem it was responding to.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-8970,
			"y":-2197,
			"width":265,
			"height":140
		},
		{
			"id":"bbebbd90befb593a",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"He really did like allegories...",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5284,
			"y":-2173,
			"width":178,
			"height":77
		},
		{
			"id":"9cb46f16a0ebdfb7",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"In fact, the True-Born Englishman was a response...\n---\nIt was mostly a response to John Tutchin's 1700 poem \"The Foreigners\", attacking the Dutch ties of the king.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-8645,
			"y":-2197,
			"width":360,
			"height":180,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"33a2613ea88d3f93",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"👥 James Knapton",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-3360,
			"y":-3360,
			"width":260,
			"height":60,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"eb31a57c3881d96b",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"The vast majority of maritime piracy was actually state sponsored for colonial rivalry purposes.\n---\nThe small minority of independent pirates took place mostly towards the end of this period.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4050,
			"y":-2363,
			"width":420,
			"height":223
		},
		{
			"id":"2bc2096f54323be2",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"What's a buccanneer?\n---\nBuccaneers were mercenaries who raided Spanish ships on semi-official behalf of the English crown.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-3790,
			"y":-2980,
			"width":460,
			"height":140
		},
		{"id":"6fc3fd90e4ff2bf8","type":"text","text":"🏴‍☠️ PYRATES?","x":-3720,"y":-2540,"width":180,"height":80,"color":"6"},
		{
			"id":"bbaa360c7384971c",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"The two ships ended up separating, and Alexander Selkirk went south with Captain Thomas Stradling, all the way to the coast of Chile.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1979,
			"y":-3286,
			"width":400,
			"height":120,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"bbc20a2e28182560",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"⏰ On 2 February 1709\n---\nThe shipwrecked Alexander Selkirk was rescued by Captain Woodes Rogers.\nAlexander was described to be in surprisingly good state.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-2380,
			"y":-2026,
			"width":330,
			"height":260,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"ea18f1cafc674a0c",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"The ship sank within days.\n---\nSome of the crew survived and was imprisoned.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-2340,
			"y":-2625,
			"width":260,
			"height":160
		},
		{
			"id":"c767cde4a3b190c0",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"https://www.google.com/maps/reviews/@56.2129711,-2.9355305,3a,31.2y,332.1h,104.39t/data=!3m7!1e1!3m5!1s7tvQIzefah1q7rzl6x5u6Q!2e0!6shttps:%2F%2Fstreetviewpixels-pa.googleapis.com%2Fv1%2Fthumbnail%3Fcb_client%3Dmaps_sv.tactile%26w%3D900%26h%3D600%26pitch%3D-14.388007490191143%26panoid%3D7tvQIzefah1q7rzl6x5u6Q%26yaw%3D332.09657819260855!7i16384!8i8192!4m6!14m5!1m4!2m3!1sChdDSUhNMG9nS0VJQ0FnSURldk0tcjVRRRAB!2m1!1s0x0:0x22de508f2ac7ea86?entry=ttu&g_ep=EgoyMDI2MDUwNi4wIKXMDSoASAFQAw%3D%3D",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":400,
			"y":114,
			"width":380,
			"height":320,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"5e81222d7e50f107",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"So historical piracy had very little to do with the ideals of individual empowerment that would later be associated with internet piracy.\n---\nIt was, by far and large, the rich getting richer.\nYou might say that states had a monopoly on piracy.\nThat would change as the ruling classes of all exploiter countries would ally, but that's a whole other story.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4050,
			"y":-2086,
			"width":420,
			"height":300
		},
		{
			"id":"91107c03708a6f6e",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"In 1703, he joined the expedition of William Dampier.\n---\nTwo ships set sail from Ireland on September 11, in search of enemy ships to destroy and loot.\n\nSelkirk was one of the navigators of the companion ship, having learnt the in-demand skills of mathematics and astronomy in his past experience.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1997,
			"y":-3626,
			"width":435,
			"height":300,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"bb96389d71c92713",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Lower Largo, Fife, Scotland, ",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1040,
			"y":-4215,
			"width":260,
			"height":147,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"fbd28305ec6e9c0f",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"He was quite a rebellious and unruly child.\n---\nHe was the seventh son of a shoemaker, which promised him a great destiny according to a folk superstition.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-2230,
			"y":-4243,
			"width":410,
			"height":175,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"a509f01839405734",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"At 17, in trouble for his behavior at church, he ran away to become a Buccaneer.\n---\nIt was a common way to make a quick buck.\nGoing back and forth to the Caribbean took around a year and a third of the crew often died in the process.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-2230,
			"y":-3954,
			"width":430,
			"height":228,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"0fe62e120bfb246d",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Alexander Selkirk looted a few riches as a navigator buccaneer, before returning to England in 1711.\n---\nHis shares of the plundered wealth appeared to amount to £800.\nBut it is his story that really captured attention.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-2159,
			"y":-1446,
			"width":440,
			"height":313,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"28d8ce86f06d2354",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"https://www.williamkinnaird.com/blog/history/alexander-selkirk-the-real-robinson-crusoe/#:~:text=There%20was%20an%20old%20superstition,be%20destined%20for%20great%20things.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1760,
			"y":-3997,
			"width":260,
			"height":172
		},
		{
			"id":"1410edb0001a9903",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Scotland was an independent nation.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1530,
			"y":-4068,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"c06caf1ce1c855b9",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Wait, how did he reconcile that with the fact that he literaly wrote fiction?\n---\nDefoe didn’t just ignore the contradiction; he built an entire philosophical defense for it. He called his method \"Religious Allegory\" or \"Parabolic Writing.\"",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-8846,
			"y":-4087,
			"width":520,
			"height":210,
			"color":"2"
		},
		{
			"id":"1c965fbb437119d7",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Woodes Rogers's only book was 1712's \"A Cruising Voyage Round the World\"\n---\nLike many sea faring captains, he published his travel journals, but his mostly garnered attention for containing the story of Alexander Selkirk.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4680,
			"y":-4082,
			"width":395,
			"height":242,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"e7fb061c2668af1b",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"👥 William Dampier\n---\nWilliam Dampier was a pauper orphan who gained notoriety through an alliance of piracy (first on his own then for the british crown) and strong interest for natural sciences and oceanography.\nHis prolific writing was a treasure trove of scientific observation and made him quite famous.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-3897,
			"y":-4380,
			"width":435,
			"height":240,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"4135463c8703a310",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"In 1703, William Dampier embarked on a life-changing expedition.\n---\nThat is where he would meet Alexander Selkirk.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-3592,
			"y":-4020,
			"width":260,
			"height":260,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"eb6050a8c4ef4d75",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"In 1697, William Dampier published \"A New Voyage Round the World\"\n---\nIt was his very first book and became an instant commercial success.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4027,
			"y":-4036,
			"width":327,
			"height":276,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"405575fbea99a505",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/55538",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4720,
			"y":-3640,
			"width":260,
			"height":60,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"fc652710331694a9",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"https://archive.org/details/newvoyageroundwo00damp/page/n17/mode/2up",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4123,
			"y":-3540,
			"width":260,
			"height":60,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"48c599ed12b05034",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Most of the revenue of course went to its publisher.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-3720,
			"y":-3560,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"f70f5c191630fe6c",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"This sounds complicated, what was piracy like at this time?\n---\nThe period between 1650 and 1730 is considered to be the golden age of piracy.\nThe very lucrative colonial trade routes (spices, slaves, luxury artifacts) started to attract robbers.\nThis is further complicated by international tensions. Spain had a headstart in the colonial race. In an effort to catch up, most nations encouraged state-sponsored piracy in order to plunder their rival's wealth.\nWhen the problem was too massive to ignore, the british navy launched a massive campaign to clean up the sea.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4502,
			"y":-2840,
			"width":585,
			"height":380,
			"color":"6"
		},
		{
			"id":"88b541cdd8c55941",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🚧 I'm sorry, believe it or not, the history of piracy is in another DLC.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4310,
			"y":-2320,
			"width":200,
			"height":133,
			"color":"3"
		},
		{
			"id":"bda37b01f524fff7",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Glorious?\n---\nHistory is written by the winners.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-7043,
			"y":-3601,
			"width":260,
			"height":120,
			"color":"6"
		},
		{
			"id":"892f81aa580d4be9",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🚧 I'm sorry, the Glorious Revolution is in another DLC",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-7043,
			"y":-3448,
			"width":260,
			"height":115,
			"color":"3"
		},
		{
			"id":"516d8b49569ae19a",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"The Shortest-Way with the Dissenters?\n---\nIt was written from the point of view of a bloodthirsty catholic extremist.\nDaniel Defoe essentially impersonated his opponents and exaggerated their positions.\nThey initially loved it, and many believed that the government was actually planning a massacre.\nWhen the truth came out, everyone was understanbly quite upset.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-7273,
			"y":-2645,
			"width":360,
			"height":390,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"939fb96ab11834bf",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"The new king was obviously quite controversial.\n---\nDaniel Defoe defended him in a satyrical poem.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6568,
			"y":-3760,
			"width":505,
			"height":134,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"09813e983375b960",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"👥 Queen Anne\nhttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anne,_Queen_of_Great_Britain",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6855,
			"y":-3093,
			"width":225,
			"height":129
		},
		{
			"id":"0fa336742d48d5a5",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"👥 King William III\nhttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_III_of_England",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6743,
			"y":-3291,
			"width":225,
			"height":129
		},
		{
			"id":"674e6a3c3e3e61d0",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"In 1703, Daniel Defoe was imprisoned for a satirical poem.\n---\nHe had published \"The Shortest-Way with the Dissenters\" anonymously but was later found out.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6825,
			"y":-2889,
			"width":400,
			"height":220,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"af6635b4e9d0d251",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"How did it compare to piracy in the sea?\n---\nFunny you should ask. The term piracy started to be extended to book copies because maritime piracy was a hot topic at the time.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4588,
			"y":-2046,
			"width":420,
			"height":180
		},
		{
			"id":"d1a142f0a782dd56",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🚧 I'm sorry, the Monmouth Rebellion is in another DLC",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6263,
			"y":-4117,
			"width":260,
			"height":115,
			"color":"3"
		},
		{
			"id":"19a8c144da17f00b",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Untill the Glorious Revolution of 1688\n---\nA group of protestant elites worried about the catholic king organized a rebellion.\nIt worked and the king's son-in-law, a protestant, acceded to the throne.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6880,
			"y":-4000,
			"width":500,
			"height":201,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"6a3eb1ddad2c0ac6",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Did Daniel Defoe read any fiction?\n---\nHe was famously suspicious of fiction, that his religious upbringing pushed him to consider \"lies told for pleasure\".\n",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-8866,
			"y":-4322,
			"width":540,
			"height":180
		},
		{
			"id":"db9af5deecdd4403",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Wow, what happened to him then?\n---\nDaniel Defoe received a pardon and continued business as usual.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6740,
			"y":-4197,
			"width":360,
			"height":161
		},
		{
			"id":"458077cf65aea0f1",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"He loved to gather information.\n---\nThat was the key to a good deal.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-9576,
			"y":-4194,
			"width":410,
			"height":120,
			"color":"6"
		},
		{
			"id":"c3f78645024410ce",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"I also think it's important to understand the world...\n---\nAnd where things come from.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-9576,
			"y":-4022,
			"width":420,
			"height":145,
			"color":"2"
		},
		{
			"id":"8676648ae26bcd81",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"💼 During his early life, Daniel Defoe was a travelling salesman.\n---\nHe rode thousands of miles all across England and Scotland.\nHe dealt in stockings (wool, silk, and cotton), but also branched out into wine, spirits, and tobacco.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-8720,
			"y":-5187,
			"width":470,
			"height":210,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"3c15c4f2632c1045",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"So... the author of Robinson Crusoe had no actual travel experience?\n---\nWell, it would appear so.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-8891,
			"y":-4704,
			"width":260,
			"height":185,
			"color":"2"
		},
		{
			"id":"82457e74613347a6",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🚢 Travel, you say?\n---\nThere is no historical record of Daniel Defoe ever setting foot outside the British Isles.\nThere are however a lot of mentions of him being an avid armchair traveler.\nHe would read everything he came across when it came to international travel.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-9316,
			"y":-5082,
			"width":300,
			"height":320,
			"color":"6"
		},
		{
			"id":"6f30d97bbab96763",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"What kind of book are we talking about here?\n---\nHe studied the Great Atlases and the journals of merchant travelers of the times.\nHe kept up to date with scientific colonial exploration.\nHe also had an interest for practical knowledge manuals, which would later give his own writing some realism and depth.\nSome names come to mind...",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-9381,
			"y":-4662,
			"width":420,
			"height":330
		},
		{
			"id":"9fa40b1636a990f3",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"👥 Woodes Rogers\n---\nWoodes Rogers was a English sea captain famous for fighting piracy in the Caribbean after having made a fortune pillaging ships on behalf of the british crown.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4680,
			"y":-4380,
			"width":395,
			"height":200,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"0960ba5e853c2d1b",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"⭐ The life of Alexander Selkirk\n---\nAlexander Selkirk was born in 1676 in a small fishing village of Scotland.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1870,
			"y":-4426,
			"width":410,
			"height":120,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"ba9f0e9a05749b1e",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"☺️ debug",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1915,
			"y":184,
			"width":480,
			"height":180,
			"color":"3"
		},
		{"id":"title_button","type":"text","text":"<h1>Push YOUR Buttons!</h1>---\nPush the ⭐ Start button!","x":2395,"y":599,"width":300,"height":200,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"start","type":"text","text":"⭐ Start\n---\nThis game is about buttons.","x":2395,"y":859,"width":300,"height":200,"color":"4"},
		{
			"id":"70e7effbed13fae3",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"theft all the way down ???",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":3035,
			"y":689,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"531817bb5f8b90ca",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Dignity?\n---\nAs opposed to the \"decadent\" birthright aristocracy.\nToo complacent.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5340,
			"y":-5459,
			"width":320,
			"height":160,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"adc2ce5c16579335",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🚰 I hope you have a source for that\n---\nPeter Earle and Maximillian E. Novak",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5070,
			"y":-5710,
			"width":380,
			"height":120
		},
		{
			"id":"7099e851dc27ad23",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🚧 I'm sorry, the history of protestantism is in another DLC",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4044,
			"y":-5172,
			"width":260,
			"height":120,
			"color":"3"
		},
		{
			"id":"0a424ad760b7b70b",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"✝️ The protestant work ethic?",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4524,
			"y":-5502,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"40eb47c3ef08be5a",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"💵 That worldview where money is a measure of salute?\n---\nMoney really is the ultimate signifier...",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4264,
			"y":-5392,
			"width":440,
			"height":140,
			"color":"6"
		},
		{
			"id":"4a3fa30454592086",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"At 25, Daniel Defoe took part in the Monmouth Rebellion\n---\nA protestant failed rebellion against the catholic king.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6510,
			"y":-4559,
			"width":260,
			"height":260,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"f9a85ef1f4484755",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"What kind of political intrigue?",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6928,
			"y":-4521,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"a331d00687a688d3",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Though the rest of his life would be full of political intrigue...",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-7280,
			"y":-4753,
			"width":420,
			"height":110,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"041706c4eac2b33b",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Very much a rebel entrepreneur of the time.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-8510,
			"y":-4789,
			"width":260,
			"height":55
		},
		{
			"id":"27a1954a4a5e8183",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"With deep ties to politics...",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-8456,
			"y":-4641,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"1364f5e6e7a345df",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"So Daniel Defoe grew up in a cultish microcosm? \n---\nHe did live in rebel, dissentious districts. ",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5540,
			"y":-5219,
			"width":360,
			"height":160,
			"color":"6"
		},
		{
			"id":"4c31c73f1f9710ad",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Wealthy bourgeois merchants, religious extremists, fallen from power...\n---\nAnd hungry for revenge?",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5590,
			"y":-5725,
			"width":460,
			"height":150,
			"color":"2"
		},
		{
			"id":"9545fa71db756f1a",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"They taught that the \"Flow of Goods\" was God’s way of bringing the world together.\n---\nProperty Rights and Contract Law were nearly as sacred as the Ten Commandments.\n\nThis gave Defoe a lifelong obsession with the \"Dignity of Trade.\"",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5420,
			"y":-6210,
			"width":360,
			"height":320,
			"color":"2"
		},
		{
			"id":"fd8affe066a584ff",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"What happened to them after the return of the monarchy?\n---\nThey had to keep their faith secret since it was illegal.\nThousands fled to America. Most stayed.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5940,
			"y":-5519,
			"width":420,
			"height":180,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"fec336acff3f8ceb",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"What's a Dissenter?\n---\nAs opposed to Conformists, Dissenters are catholic protestants who refuse the English monarch as \"pope\".\nA lot of them were in some ways refugees from the continent fleeing persecution.\nSome were of French or Flemish origins.\nThey were the religious movement underpinning the republican revolution a decade earlier, and the puritan dictatorship that succeeded.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5930,
			"y":-6130,
			"width":420,
			"height":360,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"894d8e9bcd6407da",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"What the *** is a green?\n---\nYou know? The <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Village_green\" target=\"_blank\">greens</a>! From the inclosures!",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6470,
			"y":-6040,
			"width":420,
			"height":120,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"4e956b014fce2d23",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🚧 I'm sorry, the Inclosure story is another DLC.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6360,
			"y":-5840,
			"width":200,
			"height":133,
			"color":"3"
		},
		{
			"id":"239181232a3195da",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"📍 Does the school building remain?\n---\nThe building did not survive the test of time, but \n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?client=firefox-b-d&q=Charles+Morton+Court\" target=\"_blank\">Charles Morton Court Prestige Student Housing</a> serve as a testament to its legacy.\nIn 2026, it seems to stand above a \"Papa Johns\" and a \"Tesco Express\".\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/maps/place/Prestige+Student+Living+Charles+Morton+Court/@51.5527125,-0.0872473,3a,75y,208.91h,112.2t/data=!3m7!1e1!3m5!1sO7GHLRfen4fRk_ZkWiYi4A!2e0!6shttps:%2F%2Fstreetviewpixels-pa.googleapis.com%2Fv1%2Fthumbnail%3Fcb_client%3Dmaps_sv.tactile%26w%3D900%26h%3D600%26pitch%3D-22.199785617299767%26panoid%3DO7GHLRfen4fRk_ZkWiYi4A%26yaw%3D208.90594074437794!7i16384!8i8192!4m6!3m5!1s0x48761c86b08c46fb:0xd1ae95b42a8e4ec0!8m2!3d51.5526405!4d-0.0873502!16s%2Fg%2F1vvr5q51?entry=ttu&g_ep=EgoyMDI2MDQyOS4wIKXMDSoASAFQAw%3D%3D\" target=\"_blank\">Here is Street View and Google Maps reviews.</a>\n<iframe src=\"https://www.google.com/maps/place/Prestige+Student+Living+Charles+Morton+Court/@51.5527125,-0.0872473,3a,75y,208.91h,112.2t/data=!3m7!1e1!3m5!1sO7GHLRfen4fRk_ZkWiYi4A!2e0!6shttps:%2F%2Fstreetviewpixels-pa.googleapis.com%2Fv1%2Fthumbnail%3Fcb_client%3Dmaps_sv.tactile%26w%3D900%26h%3D600%26pitch%3D-22.199785617299767%26panoid%3DO7GHLRfen4fRk_ZkWiYi4A%26yaw%3D208.90594074437794!7i16384!8i8192!4m6!3m5!1s0x48761c86b08c46fb:0xd1ae95b42a8e4ec0!8m2!3d51.5526405!4d-0.0873502!16s%2Fg%2F1vvr5q51?entry=ttu&g_ep=EgoyMDI2MDQyOS4wIKXMDSoASAFQAw%3D%3D\"></iframe>",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6480,
			"y":-6570,
			"width":400,
			"height":440,
			"color":"6"
		},
		{
			"id":"473213a9261ef550",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"A famous religious and intellectual microcosm for Dissenters.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6790,
			"y":-6320,
			"width":260,
			"height":190
		},
		{
			"id":"7ed03c78122433b8",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Daniel Defoe received the closest thing to an MBA that existed in the 17th century.\n---\nMathematics and languages as business tools.\nWith a heavy dose of religious and ethical leaning.\n",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5850,
			"y":-6550,
			"width":340,
			"height":260
		},
		{
			"id":"e953bfccfb1d94eb",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Wait wasn't it the story of a shipwreck?\n---\nThat's after.\nRobinson becomes a successful plantation owner, but he wants more.\nHe sets out on a ship and it crashes.\nHe is the only survivor on what appears to be a deserted island.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4300,
			"y":2940,
			"width":340,
			"height":260,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"15e17bf535958fc0",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"It became the archetypal story of the shipwrecked survivor.\n---\nMost subsequent stories around this trope stem from here.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4000,
			"y":3260,
			"width":260,
			"height":280
		},
		{
			"id":"1ae73135635a57db",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"The island only \"appears\" deserted?\n---\nYep, after like 15 years alone Robinson finds traces of a cannibal tribe that sometimes comes to the island for rituals.\nHe rescues one of their prisoners and befriend him.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4400,
			"y":3270,
			"width":355,
			"height":260,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"0f9b63169d44984d",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"What happens next?\n---\nThey rescue a couple more people.\nRobinson takes the leadership role.\nA ship rescues them after 28 years.\n\nThen he returns to England where he's rich from his earlier plantation.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4150,
			"y":3730,
			"width":420,
			"height":230,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"b88844f76bf4c415",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🏁 And that's the happy end of Robinson Crusoe",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4180,
			"y":4080,
			"width":435,
			"height":80,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"979e3157e14bb47f",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"What happens to Friday?\n---\nHe remains Robinson's wealthy companion and servant in England.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4195,
			"y":4240,
			"width":465,
			"height":140
		},
		{
			"id":"9476a4a57496750b",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"There's a sequel though...\n---\nThey set sail again and Friday is killed by the island \"savages\".\nThen the book is mostly Robinson's adventure travel log around the planet.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4195,
			"y":4440,
			"width":465,
			"height":180,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"51d8ca31ceeb8f55",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"⭐ Friday?\n---\nYeah, he's an indigenous native from a rival cannibal tribe. \n\nRobinson frees him and tames him with civilization.\nHe names him after the day he was rescued.\n\nThey become close friends, leading to another archetypal trope.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4800,
			"y":3730,
			"width":560,
			"height":230,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"31793240f96b9314",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"It's called \"networking\"\n---\nThe ruling class helping their owns.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5440,
			"y":-6530,
			"width":370,
			"height":110,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"cc2c941236c1540a",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Do I smell the distinctive odor of business school?",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5520,
			"y":-6760,
			"width":260,
			"height":60,
			"color":"6"
		},
		{
			"id":"061ca5ebd5e9bf0f",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"👥 Charles Morton\n---\nA pioneer intellectual who revolutionized education.\nHe ran a private academy for 20 years before moving to America.\nHe went on to have a major role in the founding of Harvard University.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6750,
			"y":-6840,
			"width":360,
			"height":220
		},
		{
			"id":"f9a56dfc6bb04b2e",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"The best education money could buy?\n---\nBack in those days, the standard prestigious institutions like Oxford or Cambridge required religious oath to the English religion, which some, like Daniel Defoe's family refused to take.\n\nInstead, they formed privately financed and operated so-called \"Dissenter\" academies, private boarding school which rapidly surpassed the old establishments in the technologies they could afford.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5960,
			"y":-7190,
			"width":420,
			"height":360,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"e1aff5886c20bec7",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"The school was shut down due to religious pressure when Defoe was a young adult.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6430,
			"y":-7190,
			"width":420,
			"height":80
		},
		{
			"id":"9e51b34e90f4962c",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Newington Green is a district in the North of London\n---\nhttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Newington_Green",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6820,
			"y":-7080,
			"width":430,
			"height":140
		},
		{
			"id":"61c6472d86377400",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"📷 This guy?\n---\nYou can see it for yourself on <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Daniel_Defoe_Kneller_Style.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Wikimedia.\n<img src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/Daniel_Defoe_Kneller_Style.jpg/960px-Daniel_Defoe_Kneller_Style.jpg\" /></a>",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6160,
			"y":-7892,
			"width":260,
			"height":400
		},
		{
			"id":"3df206418f34d8a5",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"So it usually goes with revolutions...",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-7570,
			"y":-7840,
			"width":400,
			"height":60,
			"color":"6"
		},
		{
			"id":"18924d63f3bc5fa5",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"💸 They made it!\n---\nThey were \"New Money\".\nThe American dream...",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6360,
			"y":-7372,
			"width":260,
			"height":145,
			"color":"6"
		},
		{"id":"d3b4516848b37f0d","type":"text","text":"⏰ Daniel Defoe was born in 1660\n---\nIt was a time of renewal, as Charles II restored the monarchy after a brief attempt at a progressive republic that devolved into a puritan military dictatorship.","x":-7050,"y":-8040,"width":460,"height":200,"color":"4"},
		{
			"id":"ef60b9e37b51d7da",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"So far\n---\nBut people learn...",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-7370,
			"y":-7700,
			"width":260,
			"height":60,
			"color":"2"
		},
		{
			"id":"5b4d451a709f0da1",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"👥 Daniel Defoe's family were London candle makers.\n---\nA successful bourgeois family.\nHis father was the first born in England from a puritan Flemish family fleeing religious persecution.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-7060,
			"y":-7670,
			"width":480,
			"height":200,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"21687949a41e78bc",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Daniel Defoe studied in Charles Morton’s Academy at Newington Green\n---\nIt was a brand new kind of education.\nThe best money could buy.\nIt was a very progressive education focused on knowledge for the sake of doing.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-7530,
			"y":-7150,
			"width":480,
			"height":245,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"6b75ea4ad9154a9f",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"The trope of the Noble Savage\n---\nhttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Noble_savage",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4860,
			"y":4260,
			"width":260,
			"height":200,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"60eac039fe5076ef",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Daniel Defoe did like his political parabola...",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4860,
			"y":4500,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"7c1e918ac46883f0",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"The trope of the indigenous native  cannibals\n---\nYeah, in this book all indigenous natives are cannibals.\n\nIt is not clear whether this is out of racism, sensationalism, or both.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4550,
			"y":4065,
			"width":300,
			"height":295
		},
		{
			"id":"44985cd157f4d855",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/521",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-3920,
			"y":2980,
			"width":260,
			"height":60,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"e1c0f56fcc55736b",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🏁 I guess that is the less happy end of Friday...",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4180,
			"y":4720,
			"width":435,
			"height":80
		},
		{
			"id":"cd3c8091905a0eaf",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"The trope of the desert island companion\n---\nPerhaps best exemplified by Wilson in the 2000 movie Cast Away.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4990,
			"y":4044,
			"width":260,
			"height":153
		},
		{
			"id":"22b354c87810316c",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Robinson Crusoe was arguably the most influential piece of propaganda providing a moral justification for British imperialism and colonialism.\n---\nIt was piling up on Spanish law.\nCannibalism was the main pretext used by the settlers to strip indigenous population of human rights.\nBut that's a whole other story.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4800,
			"y":4620,
			"width":502,
			"height":300,
			"color":"2"
		},
		{
			"id":"f80585c633f80684",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🚧 I'm sorry, the history of colonialism is in another DLC.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4741,
			"y":5000,
			"width":383,
			"height":80,
			"color":"3"
		},
		{
			"id":"e1ef83e94426eeac",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Robinson Crusoe is widely credited for inventing realism.\n---\nIt was one of the first times stories shifted away from fantastical legends and epic poems.\nContrary to what came before, it put the focus on the individual.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-442,
			"y":3660,
			"width":485,
			"height":222
		},
		{
			"id":"d4abd4c74c4102bd",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Earlier notable titles influential in the realist revolution of storytelling include...\n---\nAnonymous - Lazarillo de Tormes (1554)\nMateo Alemán - Guzmán de Alfarache (1599)\nMiguel de Cervantes - Don Quixote (1605)",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-417,
			"y":4013,
			"width":460,
			"height":232
		},
		{
			"id":"0abbd3fad4cdae52",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Robinson Crusoe was a major step in the history of the first person pronoun.\n---\nIt is sometimes described as the first book starting with \"I\".\nThis is slightly exaggerated, as ancient epic literature or picaresque stories sometimes used first person.\n\nIt is, however, one of the first instances of a truly ordinary, human first person narrator that shone through its individuality.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":60,
			"y":3650,
			"width":520,
			"height":232
		},
		{
			"id":"9849450ef53749ff",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Beware the Cat (1570) by William Baldwin is a notable example of earlier first person fiction.\n---\nSometimes argued to be the true first English novel, it is a satirical, first-person gothic horror-comedy about a man who creates a potion to understand the language of cats.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":160,
			"y":3929,
			"width":480,
			"height":214
		},
		{
			"id":"51bbe2b4dfdb95ad",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Robinson Crusoe was one of the first instances of identity appropriation for profit.\n---\nIt pioneered identity as a commercial product.\nOther notable examples of identity fraud/twisting reality for personal gain include...\n\nMary Carleton - The Case of Madam Mary Carleton (1663)\nAphra Behn - Oroonoko (1688)\nGeorge Psalmanazar - An Historical and Geographical Description of Formosa (1704)",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":640,
			"y":3696,
			"width":480,
			"height":340
		},
		{
			"id":"98fa05400360f86f",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Robinson Crusoe was one of the first autobiography of a fictional character.\n---\nOther notable steps in the history of fictional characters are \nApuleius - The Golden Ass (2nd Century AD)\nAnonymous - Lazarillo de Tormes (1554)\nMateo Alemán - Guzmán de Alfarache (1599)\n\nEarlier storytelling relied mostly on mythical archetypes.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-530,
			"y":4347,
			"width":590,
			"height":287
		},
		{
			"id":"2b0702bb0cdacd74",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Robinson Crusoe was one of the first instances of experimental literature.\n---\nIt was one of the first recorded conscious attempts at breaking away from established literary conventions and pushing the boundaries of the medium.\nOther notable examples include...\n\nCharles Sorel - The Comical History of Francion (1623)\nThomas Nashe - The Unfortunate Traveller (1594)\nAphra Behn - Oroonoko (1688)",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":100,
			"y":4197,
			"width":520,
			"height":300
		},
		{
			"id":"e2e99e80d2920698",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Robinson Crusoe was the first ever best-seller.\n---\nRobinson Crusoe sold out its first run of a thousand copies in a few weeks, and would sell around 5000 copies in a few months.\n\nFor comparison, it took William Dampier's journals a decade to sell this amount of copies in 1697.\n\nThe former best seller, John Bunyan's The Pilgrim's Progress (1678), took months to sell out its first run.\n\nRobinson Crusoe's record would later be broken by Jonathan Swift's Gulliver's Travels in 1726.\n\nBut the book marks an indisputable landmark in the history of commercial litterature.\n",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1260,
			"y":3636,
			"width":600,
			"height":400
		},
		{
			"id":"c016896c6491e90f",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Robinson Crusoe effectively marked the birth of commercial best selling books.\n---\nIt was, in essence, the start of the literature market.\nThis was made possible by the middle class having money and time to spare for leisure such as reading.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1260,
			"y":4100,
			"width":600,
			"height":200
		},
		{
			"id":"c76e8ade990d2e17",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Why did the middle class have money and time for leisure?\n---\nThe prosperity of the british middle class that carried this was of course due to multiple factor, including:\n- Colonial Atlantic trade, including but not limited to slavery\n- Expropriation and enclosure of common land\n- Financial revolution with the birth of joint stock companies and speculation\n- War economy, notably looting the enemy\n- Domestic manufacturing (textile, iron, coal)",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1260,
			"y":4344,
			"width":600,
			"height":356,
			"color":"2"
		},
		{
			"id":"994762b03e8ab90f",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🚧 I'm sorry, the history of all of this is in another DLC.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1369,
			"y":4745,
			"width":383,
			"height":80,
			"color":"3"
		},
		{
			"id":"c246bd471dab70ba",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Robinson Crusoe was a major step in the history of marketing and PR.\n---\nDaniel Defoe did not pioneer realism out of love for experimental art, but because he thought it would be a good commercial strategy, and it worked.\n\nOther comparable successful marketing schemes include...\n\nIn the 1660s, traveling showmen doctors known as montebanks would perform miracles on stage, blurring the line between entertainment and marketing.\n\nIn the 1670s, William Salmon made a fortune selling mysterious medicine thanks to dubious testimonials he published in.\n\nIn 1708 Jonathan Swift drove his competitor John Partridge out of business by publishing fake news of his death.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":253,
			"y":4664,
			"width":655,
			"height":423
		},
		{"id":"e100bf5300059e3b","type":"text","text":"That's a very long title...\n---\nIt was the norm at the time.\nBooks were sold unbound and the binding chosen separately, so this was essentially the cover meant to convince potential buyers.\nPretty much their equivalent of clickbait.","x":-2440,"y":3440,"width":680,"height":174},
		{
			"id":"14b4dd84724217a2",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Their clickbait had a lot more words than ours.\n---\nI would include a reactionary \"kids these days don't read\", but I think it's mostly because reproducing images was hard.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-2178,
			"y":3660,
			"width":443,
			"height":220,
			"color":"6"
		},
		{
			"id":"e7ae7559b547b87f",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🚧 I'm sorry, the history of marketing and PR is in the Edward Bernays DLC.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":389,
			"y":5180,
			"width":383,
			"height":80,
			"color":"3"
		},
		{"id":"bcc0cef568d3e4b0","type":"text","text":"Wait... written by himself?\n---\nYes, the story was first published as if it was written by Robinson Crusoe himself.\n\nWe might call this fraud, but most customers at the time it came out genuinely thought Robinson Crusoe was a real person.\n\nFortunately, the story it plagiarized was also quite popular so it did not take too long for the truth to come out.","x":-2920,"y":3574,"width":380,"height":306},
		{
			"id":"4b528aca22c67388",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Robinson Crusoe is based on the real life of Alexander Selkirk.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-3930,
			"y":5840,
			"width":400,
			"height":80
		},
		{
			"id":"a9d193d351e657e6",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Daniel Defoe would keep up the pretense until his death.\n---\nHe kept publishing notes as Robinson Crusoe to further entrench the deception.\n\nHis name would not be officially associated with the book until 50 years later, when the story was finally advertised as fiction.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-2745,
			"y":5280,
			"width":620,
			"height":260
		},
		{
			"id":"6aa5f04540243864",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"The line between punishable fraud and revolutionary breakthrough seems to be somewhere between your bank account and your skin color.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1765,
			"y":5123,
			"width":340,
			"height":166,
			"color":"6"
		},
		{
			"id":"f1675312d7efb6e0",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Robinson Crusoe being marketed as a \"true\" autobiography was a deliberate advertisement strategy from its author.\n---\nThe realism literary style was not born out of love for experimentation but from a cold and calculated commercial strategy.\n\nThe myriad of details in the book served to ground the fundamental deception that it was completely made-up.\n\nBack then like now, authenticity (or more precisely its appearance) sells.\n\nSensationalist exaggerations (like the cannibalism) were also used to boost the sales.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1857,
			"y":4143,
			"width":510,
			"height":431,
			"color":"2"
		},
		{
			"id":"f028389b9abdd9d4",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🏁 And that was the other end of Robinson Crusoe",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-2613,
			"y":5720,
			"width":435,
			"height":80,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"66c4f6ec942482de",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"You might say that this little marketing trick caused a reality crisis.\n---\nMedia were in their infancy and so was media literacy.\nMost readers thought they were getting the latest news from the colonies.\n\nSome critics like Charles Gildon or John Clavel were outraged at the idea of using deliberate misinformation for profit.\n\nIn fact, Daniel Defoe got into a bit of legal trouble as other people claimed the fake story for themselves.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-2767,
			"y":4660,
			"width":589,
			"height":351,
			"color":"6"
		}
	],
	"edges":[
		{"id":"0e839c2e-8cd3-4de9-ba1d-b1e5b222b8c8","fromNode":"title","fromSide":"bottom","toNode":"title_aside","toSide":"top"},
		{"id":"dae756de-8db4-429b-a745-8febe1e91f61","fromNode":"title_aside","fromSide":"bottom","toNode":"title_aside2","toSide":"top"},
		{"id":"2687df4f-7479-4db4-9b50-402fb684edaa","fromNode":"title_aside","fromSide":"bottom","toNode":"49209c0bf5486f14","toSide":"top"},
		{"id":"d64567f7-0f5c-4ab6-9baf-e8ff69d09b3c","fromFloating":false,"fromNode":"d0c1f86e10f949da","fromSide":"bottom","toNode":"f919133328a5e0d3","toSide":"top","color":"5"},
		{"id":"8bd4bb08-3d4a-41f3-8242-cdb334466b18","fromFloating":false,"fromNode":"d0c1f86e10f949da","fromSide":"bottom","toNode":"d674c3e21ca88716","toSide":"top","color":"4"},
		{"id":"9e0d1bf4-2845-4e75-83d0-6c146e8ee904","fromFloating":false,"fromNode":"d0c1f86e10f949da","fromSide":"bottom","toNode":"e8a4775851bc5327","toSide":"top","color":"6"},
		{"id":"52cd36ea8d4ef506","fromFloating":false,"fromNode":"d674c3e21ca88716","fromSide":"bottom","toNode":"d3b4516848b37f0d","toSide":"top","color":"4"},
		{"id":"f06bffbc-d1fe-46e1-a6de-df010c028da7","toFloating":false,"fromNode":"e8a4775851bc5327","fromSide":"bottom","toNode":"e100bf5300059e3b","toSide":"top"},
		{"id":"aa5f385a-3d84-4748-8616-bec943d8aae7","fromNode":"e8a4775851bc5327","fromSide":"bottom","toNode":"bcc0cef568d3e4b0","toSide":"top"},
		{"id":"7045d400115a7f8e","fromNode":"e8a4775851bc5327","fromSide":"bottom","toNode":"d59c4d2f4f4674df","toSide":"top"},
		{"id":"12c103b1ee1807ae","toFloating":false,"fromNode":"e8a4775851bc5327","fromSide":"bottom","toNode":"6fc3fd90e4ff2bf8","toSide":"top"},
		{"id":"62b7eb7bf325f983","fromNode":"e8a4775851bc5327","fromSide":"bottom","toNode":"72d5074d19b63713","toSide":"top"},
		{"id":"8dd48722b8762bed","fromNode":"72d5074d19b63713","fromSide":"bottom","toNode":"ed26200129d229b8","toSide":"top"},
		{"id":"679b79ddacbb271e","fromNode":"72d5074d19b63713","fromSide":"bottom","toNode":"99677d0088771e0d","toSide":"top"},
		{
			"id":"88b263b016db99dd",
